`getEstimatedTickCount` does not guard against NaN propagation. If either domain date is invalid, `maxTime - minTime` evaluates to `NaN`. `NaN / intervalMs` is `NaN`, `Math.ceil(NaN)` is `NaN`, and the critical comparison `NaN > MAX_TICK_COUNT` evaluates to `false`. The guard is silently bypassed and the invalid tick interval reaches the switch statement — exactly the crash scenario this PR aims to prevent.

Fix by explicitly checking for non-finite or zero interval before dividing:
```js
const timeDiffMs = maxTime - minTime;
const intervalMs = dayjs.duration({ [interval ?? 'day']: every }).asMilliseconds();
if (!isFinite(timeDiffMs) || !intervalMs) {
  return Infinity; // forces the guard to clamp ticks
}
return Math.ceil(timeDiffMs / intervalMs);
```
